Why This Is Asked
Tests practical error handling and DLQ routing in KCNA pipelines, a common real-world requirement. Emphasizes per-topic isolation, backpressure, and safe offset management.

Key Concepts
- Dead-letter routing per topic
- Retry policy with backoff and max attempts
- Offset commits tied to successful processing
- Canary validation of DLQ behavior and downstream progress

Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(msg) {
  try {
    const payload = parse(msg.value);
    if (!isValid(payload)) throw new Error('invalid payload');
    process(payload);
    commitOffsets(msg); // on success
  } catch (e) {
    routeToDL(msg, e.message); // to sensor_DL or alerts_DL based on topic
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor DLQ growth and set alert thresholds?
- What strategies prevent DLQ messages from growing unbounded while ensuring no data loss in the main path?

Why This Is Asked
- Validates per-tenant auditable trails without impacting throughput
- Ensures tamper evidence via signatures and secure storage
- Checks ability to query across tenants with predictable retention

Key Concepts
- Append-only logs, tenant isolation, data integrity, retention strategy
- Tamper detection using MACs or signatures, and secure storage backends
- API design for write/read with minimal surface area and clear semantics

Code Example
```javascript
class AuditLog {
  constructor(storage, signer) {
    this.storage = storage; // per-tenant append-only store
    this.signer = signer;   // cryptographic signing service
  }

  async write(tenant, event) {
    const entry = {
      timestamp: Date.now(),
      tenant,
      event,
      signature: await this.signer.sign(event)
    };
    return await this.storage.append(tenant, entry);
  }

  async query(tenant, since, limit) {
    const entries = await this.storage.read(tenant, since, limit);
    return entries.filter(entry => 
      this.signer.verify(entry.event, entry.signature)
    );
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

To assess practical integration of KMS-based encryption, tenant isolation, and auditability in KCNA at scale, including key rotation, revocation, and observability under load.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant KEK
- Envelope encryption
- Zero-downtime key rotation
- Tenant isolation proofs
- Auditability and proofs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: fetch KEK, generate data key, encrypt payload, store ciphertext and wrapped key
async function sealPayload(tenantId, payload, kms){
  const KEK = await kms.getKEK(tenantId);
  const DEK = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));
  const wrappedDEK = await wrapKeyWithKEK(DEK, KEK);
  const ciphertext = await encrypt(payload, DEK);
  return { ciphertext, wrappedDEK };
}
```

