## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to join across topics with late-arriving updates, implement a TTL-based in-memory state store, and maintain at-least-once semantics while isolating enrichment logic.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-topic enrichment and per-key state
- TTL-based eviction and stale data handling
- Out-of-order arrival tolerance and idempotent processing
- Error routing to dead_letters and offset management

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: in-memory profile store with TTL
class ProfileStore {
  constructor(ttlMs) { this.ttlMs = ttlMs; this.map = new Map(); }
  update(userId, profile) { this.map.set(userId, { profile, t: Date.now() }); }
  get(userId) {
    const e = this.map.get(userId); if (!e) return null;
    if (Date.now() - e.t > this.ttlMs) { this.map.delete(userId); return null; }
    return e.profile;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test eviction and data freshness without data loss?
- How would you simulate out-of-order profile updates in tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about data deletion in log-based, multi-tenant systems; covers tombstone propagation, privacy guarantees, auditability, and verification under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Tombstone semantics, cross-topic propagation, and per-tenant isolation
- Idempotence, retention versus deletion semantics
- Audit trails and verification under failure conditions
- Handling cross-topic joins while preserving deletion markers
- Compression-aware deletion propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
function createTombstone(tenantId, key, deletionTimestamp) {
  return {
    tenantId,
    key,
    deletionTimestamp,
    isTombstone: true
  };
}

function propagateTombstone(tombstone, pipeline) {
  pipeline.forEach(stage => {
    stage.emit(tombstone);
    stage.compress(); // Preserve tombstone through compression
  });
}
```

